<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract">
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">We present a unifying approach to derive the strong spatial mixing (SSM) property for the general 2-spin system from zero-free regions of its partition function.
Our approach works for the <em id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">multivariate</em> partition function over all three <em id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">complex</em> parameters <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math>, and we allow the zero-free regions of <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta,\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta,\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> to be of arbitrary shapes.
As long as the zero-free region contains a positive point and it is
a complex neighborhood of <math id="abstract1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=0</annotation></semantics></math> when fixing <math id="abstract1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta,\gamma\in\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta,\gamma\in\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, or a complex neighborhood of <math id="abstract1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\gamma=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\gamma=1</annotation></semantics></math> when fixing <math id="abstract1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta,\lambda\in\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta,\lambda\in\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="abstract1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma,\lambda\in\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma,\lambda\in\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math> respectively, we are able to show that the corresponding 2-spin system exhibits SSM on such a region.
The underlying graphs of the 2-spin system are <em id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">not</em> necessarily of bounded degree, while are required to include graphs with pinned vertices.
We prove this result by establishing a Christoffel-Darboux type identity for the 2-spin system on trees.
This identity plays an important role in our approach and is of its own interests.
We also use certain tools from complex analysis such as Riemann mapping theorem.</p>
    
<p id="abstract1.2" class="ltx_p">Our approach comprehensively turns all existing zero-free regions (to our best knowledge) of the partition function of the 2-spin system where pinned vertices are allowed into the SSM property.
As a consequence, we obtain new SSM results for the 2-spin system beyond the direct argument for SSM based on tree recurrence.
Moreover, we extend our approach to handle the 2-spin system with non-uniform external fields.
As an application, we obtain a new SSM result for the non-uniform ferromagnetic Ising model from the celebrated Lee-Yang circle theorem.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Spin systems arise from statistical physics to model interactions between
neighbors on graphs.
In this paper, we consider the 2-spin system.
Such a system is defined on a simple undirected graph <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=(V,E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=(V,E)</annotation></semantics></math> and in this way the individual entities comprising the system correspond to the
vertices <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> and their pairwise interactions correspond to the edges <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math>.
The system is associated with three parameters including two edge activities <math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> that model the tendency of vertices to agree and disagree with their neighbors, and a vertex activity <math id="S1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> that models a uniform external field which determines the propensity of a vertex to be the spin <math id="S1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math>.
A partial configuration of this system refers to a mapping <math id="S1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma:\Lambda\to\{+,-\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">+</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma:\Lambda\to\{+,-\}</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda\subseteq V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda\subseteq V</annotation></semantics></math> which may be empty. It assigns one of the two spins <math id="S1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math> to each vertex in <math id="S1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math>.
When <math id="S1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda=V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda=V</annotation></semantics></math>, it is a configuration and its weight denoted by <math id="S1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta^{m_{+}(\sigma)}\gamma^{m_{-}(\sigma)}\lambda^{n_{+}(\sigma)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>β</mi><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>γ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta^{m_{+}(\sigma)}\gamma^{m_{-}(\sigma)}\lambda^{n_{+}(\sigma)}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{+}(\sigma),m_{-}(\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{+}(\sigma),m_{-}(\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{+}(\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{+}(\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> denote respectively the number of <math id="S1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(+,+)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">+</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">+</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(+,+)</annotation></semantics></math> edges, <math id="S1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(-,-)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(-,-)</annotation></semantics></math> edges and vertices with the spin <math id="S1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math>.
The <em id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">partition function</em> of a 2-spin system is defined to be</p>
<table id="S1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="Z_{G}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda):=\sum\limits_{\sigma:V\to\{+,-\}}w(\sigma)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">+</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mi>w</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{G}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda):=\sum\limits_{\sigma:V\to\{+,-\}}w(\sigma).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">We also define the partition function conditioning on a pre-described partial configuration <math id="S1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\Lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\Lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e., each vertex in <math id="S1.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, called pinned vertex is pinned to be the spin <math id="S1.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S1.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>) denoted by <math id="S1.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> to be <math id="S1.p1.m26" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\sum_{\sigma:V\to\{+,-\}\atop\sigma|_{\Lambda}=\sigma_{\Lambda}}w(\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">+</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{\sigma:V\to\{+,-\}\atop\sigma|_{\Lambda}=\sigma_{\Lambda}}w(\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S1.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma|_{\Lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma|_{\Lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the restriction of the configuration <math id="S1.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S1.p1.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Computing the partition function of the 2-spin system given an input graph <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is a very basic counting problem, and it is known to be #P-hard for all complex valued parameters <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math>
but a few very restricted settings such as <math id="S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\gamma=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\gamma=1</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=0</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bar82</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">CCL13</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx10" title="" class="ltx_ref">CLX14</a>]</cite>.
Many natural combinatorial problems can be formulated as computing the partition function of the 2-spin system.
For example, when <math id="S1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{G}(0,1,\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{G}(0,1,\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> is the independence polynomial of the graph <math id="S1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>
(also known as the <em id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">hard-core model</em> in statistical physics); it counts the number of independent
sets of the graph <math id="S1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=1</annotation></semantics></math>.
And when <math id="S1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>, such a 2-spin system is the famous <em id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Ising model</em>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In classical statistical mechanics the parameters <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> are usually non-negative real numbers and <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\beta,\gamma)\neq(0,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\beta,\gamma)\neq(0,0)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Such 2-spin systems are divided into the <em id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">ferromagnetic</em> case (<math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\gamma&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\gamma&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>) and the <em id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">antiferromagnetic</em> case (<math id="S1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\gamma&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\gamma&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>).
For non-negative <math id="S1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> that are not all zeros,
the partition function can be viewed as the normalizing factor of the Gibbs distribution,
which is the distribution where a configuration <math id="S1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is drawn with probability
<math id="S1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm Pr}_{G;\beta,\gamma,\lambda}(\sigma)=\frac{w(\sigma)}{Z_{G}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Pr</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm Pr}_{G;\beta,\gamma,\lambda}(\sigma)=\frac{w(\sigma)}{Z_{G}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)}</annotation></semantics></math>.
However, it is meaningful to consider parameters of complex values.
First, the parameters are generally complex valued for quantum computation.
For instance, the partition function of 2-spin systems with complex parameters is closely related to the output probability amplitudes of quantum circuits <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx11" title="" class="ltx_ref">DDVM11</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx24" title="" class="ltx_ref">ICBB14</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx33" title="" class="ltx_ref">MB19</a>]</cite>.
Moreover, even in classical theory, the study of the location of <em id="S1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">complex</em> zeros of the partition function <math id="S1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{G}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{G}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> connects closely to the analyticity of the free energy <math id="S1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log Z_{G}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log Z_{G}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math>, which is a classical notion in statistical physics for defining and understanding the phenomenon of <em id="S1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">phase transitions</em>.
One of the first and also the best known results regarding the zeros of the partition function is the Lee-Yang theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx47" title="" class="ltx_ref">YL52</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx32" title="" class="ltx_ref">LY52</a>]</cite> for the Ising model.
This result was later extended to more general models by several people <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Asa70</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rue71</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx21" title="" class="ltx_ref">GS73</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx34" title="" class="ltx_ref">New74</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx28" title="" class="ltx_ref">LS81</a>]</cite>.
Another standard notion for formalizing phase transitions in the 2-spin system is <em id="S1.p3.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">correlation decay</em>, which refers to that correlations between spins decay exponentially with the distance between them.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The two notions of phase transitions can also be exploited directly to devise fully polynomial-time deterministic approximation schemes (FPTAS) for computing the partition function of the 2-spin system.
The method associated with correlation decay, or more precisely <em id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">strong spatial mixing</em> (SSM)<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
            
            
            
          There are weak spatial mixing and strong spatial mixing.
Weak spatial mixing just refers to the correlation decay property;
strong spatial mixing requires that correlations between spins decay exponentially with distance even when conditioning on a pre-described partial configuration.</span></span></span>
was originally developed by Weitz <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei06</a>]</cite> for the hard-core model.
It turns out to be a very powerful tool for antiferromagnetic 2-spin systems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx48" title="" class="ltx_ref">ZLB11</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx26" title="" class="ltx_ref">LLY12</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx27" title="" class="ltx_ref">LLY13</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx45" title="" class="ltx_ref">SST14</a>]</cite>.
While for ferromagnetic 2-spin systems, limited results <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx48" title="" class="ltx_ref">ZLB11</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx19" title="" class="ltx_ref">GL18</a>]</cite> have been obtained via the correlation decay method.
The method turning complex zero-free regions of the partition function into FPTASes was developed by Barvinok <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bar16</a>]</cite>, and extended by Patel and Regts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx36" title="" class="ltx_ref">PR17</a>]</cite>. It is usually called the <em id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Taylor polynomial interpolation</em> method.
Motivated by this method, several complex zero-free regions have been obtained for hard-core models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx37" title="" class="ltx_ref">PR19</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">BCSV23</a>]</cite>, Ising models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx29" title="" class="ltx_ref">LSS19a</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx38" title="" class="ltx_ref">PR20</a>]</cite>, and general 2-spin systems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx20" title="" class="ltx_ref">GLL20</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx44" title="" class="ltx_ref">SS21</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Below, we give a table to summarize known zero-freeness results for 2-spin systems.
For a set <math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\subseteq\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\subseteq\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, we use <math id="S1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(I)</annotation></semantics></math> to denote a complex neighborhood of <math id="S1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>.
The forth column (named after “Pinned”) refers to whether the zero-freeness result holds for graphs with pinned vertices or not.
The fifth column (named after “Bounded”) refers to whether the zero-freeness result holds only for graphs of degree bounded by some integer or not.
The result in Row 7 is the celebrated Lee-Yang theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx32" title="" class="ltx_ref">LY52</a>]</cite>.
For zero-free regions in Rows 1, 2, 4 and 6, the correlation decay property is also known to be hold on these regions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx23" title="" class="ltx_ref">HSV18</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx44" title="" class="ltx_ref">SS21</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
