<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Remarkably, the two notions of phase transitions zero-freeness and correlation decay show some inherent connections.
In seminal works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx13" title="" class="ltx_ref">DS85</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx14" title="" class="ltx_ref">DS87</a>]</cite>, Dobrushin and Shlosman proved that the correlation decay property is actually equivalent to the analyticity of the free energy
and zero-freeness of the partition function for the special case of lattices.
Recently, some more refined connections between them have been observed in the hard-core model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx37" title="" class="ltx_ref">PR19</a>]</cite>, the Ising model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx29" title="" class="ltx_ref">LSS19a</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx38" title="" class="ltx_ref">PR20</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx31" title="" class="ltx_ref">LSS22</a>]</cite>, and the general 2-spin system <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx44" title="" class="ltx_ref">SS21</a>]</cite> on graphs of bounded degree.
In these results, one can extend real parameters <math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> in which correlation decay exists to their complex neighborhoods where the partition function is zero-free.
However, it is not known whether correlation decay or SSM directly implies zero-freeness of the partition function.
On the other hand, Gamarnik <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gam23</a>]</cite> showed that zero-freeness of the partition function
directly implies a weak form of SSM for the hard-core model and other
graph homomorphism models on graphs with certain properties.
Later, Regts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Reg23</a>]</cite> showed that zero-freeness in fact implies SSM for the hard-core model and other
graph homomorphism models on all graphs of bounded degree.</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Our contributions</h3>

<div id="S1.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we further study the direction from zero-freeness of the partition function to obtain SSM for the general 2-spin system.
We show that zero-freeness of the partition function implies SSM for 2-spin systems on arbitrary graphs.
Unlike the result for the hard-core model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Reg23</a>]</cite> where the partition function is a univariate function on <math id="S1.SSx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> and the zero-free region of <math id="S1.SSx1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is in a strip shape, in this paper we view the partition function <math id="S1.SSx1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{G}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{G}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> of the 2-spin system, as a <em id="S1.SSx1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">multivariate</em> function over three <em id="S1.SSx1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">complex</em> parameters <math id="S1.SSx1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math>, and we allow the zero-free regions of <math id="S1.SSx1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta,\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta,\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S1.SSx1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> to be of arbitrary shapes as long as they contain some particular points.
For such zero-free regions, we are able to show SSM holds.
Below, we formally describe our results.
A region refers to a standard complex region i.e., a simply connected open set in <math id="S1.SSx1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, and a neighborhood of some <math id="S1.SSx1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\in\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\in\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math> refers to a standard complex neighborhood, i.e., a region containing <math id="S1.SSx1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math>.
For <math id="S1.SSx1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\in\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\in\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, we say <math id="S1.SSx1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subseteq\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subseteq\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math> is a (complex) quasi-neighborhood of <math id="S1.SSx1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> if either <math id="S1.SSx1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> is a neighborhood of <math id="S1.SSx1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S1.SSx1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U=\{z\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U=\{z\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For a region <math id="S1.SSx1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S1.SSx1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\notin U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>∉</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\notin U</annotation></semantics></math>, we define <math id="S1.SSx1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/U</annotation></semantics></math> to be the set <math id="S1.SSx1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{z\in\mathbb{C}\mid 1/z\in U\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{z\in\mathbb{C}\mid 1/z\in U\}</annotation></semantics></math> which is still a region.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> be
a family of graphs, <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{1},U_{2},U_{3}\subseteq\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{1},U_{2},U_{3}\subseteq\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math> be one of the following cases:</span></p>
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{2}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> are quasi-neighborhoods of some </span><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{0},\gamma_{0}{\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{0},\gamma_{0}{\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> respectively where </span><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{0},\gamma_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{0},\gamma_{0}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> are not both zeros, and
</span><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{3}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a neighborhood of </span><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">;</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;padding-top:-2.2pt;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{2}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{3}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> are quasi-neighborhoods of some </span><math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{0},\lambda_{0}{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{0},\lambda_{0}{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> respectively where </span><math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\notin U_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>∉</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\notin U_{2}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, and </span><math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a neighborhood of </span><math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/U_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/U_{2}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;padding-top:-2.2pt;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{3}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> are quasi-neighborhoods of some </span><math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{0},\lambda_{0}{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{0},\lambda_{0}{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> respectively where </span><math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\notin U_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>∉</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\notin U_{1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, and </span><math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{2}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a neighborhood of </span><math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/U_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/U_{1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Denote <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(U_{1}\times U_{2}\backslash\{(0,0)\})\times(U_{3}\backslash\{0\})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(U_{1}\times U_{2}\backslash\{(0,0)\})\times(U_{3}\backslash\{0\})</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐔</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Suppose that for any graph <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\in\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\in\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> and any feasible partial configuration <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\Lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\Lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> (Definition <a href="#Thmtheorem6" title="Definition 6 (Feasible partial configuration). ‣ 2.1 Notations and definitions ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>), the partition function <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> is zero-free on <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐔</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, for any <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)\in\mathbf{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝐔</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)\in\mathbf{U}</annotation></semantics></math>, the 2-spin system defined on <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> with parameters <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> exhibits SSM.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Moreover, when <math id="Thmtheorem1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> is
a family of graphs of bounded degree, the parameters <math id="Thmtheorem1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{0},\gamma_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{0},\gamma_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> in conditions 2 and 3 can be relaxed to any nonzero complex numbers.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SSx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SSx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In particular, for the Ising model, we have the following SSM result from zero-free regions of <math id="S1.SSx1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> be
a family of graphs, <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> be a nonzero complex number, <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> be a neighborhood of <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Suppose that for any graph <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\in\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\in\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> and any feasible partial configuration <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\Lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\Lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>, the partition function <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,\lambda_{0}}(\beta)=Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G}(\beta,\beta,\lambda_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,\lambda_{0}}(\beta)=Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G}(\beta,\beta,\lambda_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> of the Ising model is zero-free on <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\in U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\in U</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, for any <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\in U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\in U</annotation></semantics></math>, the corresponding Ising model specified by <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> exhibits SSM.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SSx1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SSx1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Roughly speaking, the first condition of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ Our contributions ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> requires that the partition function viewed as a function on <math id="S1.SSx1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is zero-free in a complex neighborhood of <math id="S1.SSx1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=0</annotation></semantics></math>, and the second and third conditions of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ Our contributions ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> together require that the partition function viewed as a function on <math id="S1.SSx1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S1.SSx1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is zero-free in a neighborhood of <math id="S1.SSx1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\gamma=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\gamma=1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Although we give three different conditions regarding different parameters <math id="S1.SSx1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda,\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda,\beta</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S1.SSx1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> for the 2-spin system and a condition for the Ising model under which zero-freeness implies SSM, they are all proved by a simple and unified approach in which a Christoffel-Darboux type identity plays a key role.
A Christoffel-Darboux type identity was originally presented for the independence polynomial on trees <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gut91</a>]</cite> and was extended to general graphs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ben18</a>]</cite> recently.
Our main technical contribution in this paper is to
establish a Christoffel-Darboux type identity to the 2-spin system on trees.
Such an identity gives a relation for the partition functions of the 2-spin system with different pinned vertices, and might be of independent interests.
Given a graph <math id="S1.SSx1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, a partial configuration <math id="S1.SSx1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\Lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\Lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S1.SSx1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u,v\in V(G)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u,v\in V(G),</annotation></semantics></math> we define</p>
