<section id="S1.SSx2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Relations with the previous result</h3>

<div id="S1.SSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We simply explain why Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3. ‣ Our contributions ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> gives a powerful tool for our approach and compare our approach with <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Reg23</a>]</cite>.
More details will be given in the proof outline (Subsection <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Proof outline ‣ 3 Zero-freeness implies SSM for 𝜆 ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>).
A key step in deriving SSM from zero-freeness is to show that the coefficients of the first <math id="S1.SSx2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> items in the Taylor series of the rational function <math id="S1.SSx2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)=\frac{Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda},+}_{G,v}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)}{Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">+</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)=\frac{Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda},+}_{G,v}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)}{Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)}</annotation></semantics></math> near some particular point (for example <math id="S1.SSx2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=0</annotation></semantics></math>) depend only on the <math id="S1.SSx2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-neighborhood of <math id="S1.SSx2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S1.SSx2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, defined as LDC (Definition <a href="#Thmtheorem15" title="Definition 15 (Local dependence of coefficients). ‣ 3.2 Local dependence of coefficients and a uniform bound on a circle ‣ 3 Zero-freeness implies SSM for 𝜆 ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>).
In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Reg23</a>]</cite>, LDC was introduced and proved for the hard-core model using the cluster expansion formula for polymer models.
However, the 2-spin system on general graphs is not known to be expressible as polymer models except for the case that <math id="S1.SSx2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=1</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S1.SSx2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=1</annotation></semantics></math>.
This seems to be an intrinsic obstacle for using cluster expansion to prove LDC for the general 2-spin system.
However, by Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3. ‣ Our contributions ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and basic calculations, we can factor out <math id="S1.SSx2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda^{d(u,v)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda^{d(u,v)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SSx2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\beta\gamma-1)^{d(u,v)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\beta\gamma-1)^{d(u,v)}</annotation></semantics></math>
from <math id="S1.SSx2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)-P_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda},u^{+}}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)-P_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda},u^{+}}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S1.SSx2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda},u^{+}}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)=\frac{Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda},+,+}_{G,v,u}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)}{Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda},+}_{G,u}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">+</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">+</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">+</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda},u^{+}}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)=\frac{Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda},+,+}_{G,v,u}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)}{Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda},+}_{G,u}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, by pinning vertices pointwise, we can eventually prove LDC for the 2-spin system both near <math id="S1.SSx2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SSx2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\gamma=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\gamma=1</annotation></semantics></math> in a unified way.
Thus, Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3. ‣ Our contributions ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> gives a powerful tool to handle the multivariate partition function of 2-spin systems and derive SSM from zero-freeness uniformly for all the three parameters <math id="S1.SSx2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> under different zero-free conditions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SSx2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SSx2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Furthermore, the application of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3. ‣ Our contributions ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> has another potential advantage. It may answer a very interesting question: <em id="S1.SSx2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">if the partition function is zero-free on a complex neighborhood of some points besides <math id="S1.SSx2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SSx2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\gamma=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\gamma=1</annotation></semantics></math>, can we still derive SSM from such zero-free regions?</em> The answer would be true if we could factor out some <math id="S1.SSx2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\lambda-\lambda_{0})^{d(u,v)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\lambda-\lambda_{0})^{d(u,v)}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S1.SSx2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\beta\gamma-z_{0})^{d(u,v)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\beta\gamma-z_{0})^{d(u,v)}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S1.SSx2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)-P_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda},u^{+}}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)-P_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda},u^{+}}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math>. However, such a factorization is false by Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3. ‣ Our contributions ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.
Thus, it is highly possible that by using Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3. ‣ Our contributions ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we have indeed carved out all zero-free regions from which SSM can be derived (at least via the approach of establishing LDC).
We also use normal functions and Montel theorem that are originally used in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Reg23</a>]</cite>, while we apply them to multivariate functions which are somewhat more complicated.
In addition, we initially use Riemann mapping theorem to deal with zero-free regions of arbitrary shapes, instead of a strip-shaped neighborhood of a real interval.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SSx3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Applications of our approach</h3>

<div id="S1.SSx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The zero-freeness conditions in
Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ Our contributions ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2. ‣ Our contributions ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> are general enough to cover all currently known zero-free regions (to our best knowledge) of the partition function of the 2-spin system with pinned vertices (the results in Rows 1 to 6 of Table <a href="#S1.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>).
For zero-free regions in Rows 3 and 5 of Table <a href="#S1.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, previously no SSM results were known, and it is not known whether a recurrence based argument that is commonly used to prove SSM directly can be easily achieved.
Thus,
we present a unifying approach to obtain SSM from zero-freeness, and indeed new SSM results beyond the recurrence method as a consequence of our approach.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 4</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For the zero-freeness results in Table <a href="#S1.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> where pinned vertices are allowed (Rows 1 to 6), SSM holds for the 2-spin system under those settings.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SSx3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SSx3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We also extend our approach to handle the 2-spin system with non-uniform external fields. As an application,
we give the following SSM result for the non-uniform ferromagnetic Ising model by extending the celebrated Lee-Yang circle theorem to graphs with pinned vertices.<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
              
              
              
            The implication from zero-freeness to SSM requires that zero-freeness holds for all graphs with pinned vertices. Without this condition, the implication is not true. In fact, for the three-dimensional Ising model at low temperatures where Lee-Yang theorem holds, it is known that SSM does not exist <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bas07</a>]</cite>, although weakly spatial mixing exists.</span></span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 5</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fix <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> and integer <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>.
The ferromagnetic Ising model on graphs of degree at most <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> specified by <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> and non-uniform complex external fields <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\lambda}=(\lambda_{v})_{v\in V(G)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝛌</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\lambda}=(\lambda_{v})_{v\in V(G)}</annotation></semantics></math> with the same argument <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e. <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\arg(\lambda_{v})=\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\arg(\lambda_{v})=\theta</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V(G)</annotation></semantics></math>) exhibits SSM if <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\inf_{v\in V(G)}|\lambda_{v}|&gt;\beta^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>inf</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><msup><mi>β</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\inf_{v\in V(G)}|\lambda_{v}|&gt;\beta^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sup_{v\in V(G)}|\lambda_{v}|&lt;1/\beta^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>sup</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>β</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sup_{v\in V(G)}|\lambda_{v}|&lt;1/\beta^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In particular, for a uniform <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, SSM holds if <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\lambda|&gt;\beta^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><msup><mi>β</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\lambda|&gt;\beta^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;|\lambda|&lt;1/\beta^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>β</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;|\lambda|&lt;1/\beta^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then by Weitz’s algorithm, there is an FPTAS for computing its partition function.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SSx3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SSx3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For the ferromagnetic Ising model, a <em id="S1.SSx3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">fully polynomial-time randomized approximation scheme</em> (FPRAS) based on Markov chain Monte Carlo (MCMC) for all graphs, all <math id="S1.SSx3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> and all positive non-uniform external fields <math id="S1.SSx3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{v}\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{v}\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> for all vertices <math id="S1.SSx3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S1.SSx3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\lambda_{v}\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\lambda_{v}\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math> for all vertices <math id="S1.SSx3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> has been long known <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx25" title="" class="ltx_ref">JS93</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx18" title="" class="ltx_ref">GJP03</a>]</cite>.
Recently, based on the zero-freeness method, an FPTAS was obtained for the ferromagnetic Ising model with a complex uniform <math id="S1.SSx3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> on graphs of bounded degree except for the Lee-Yang circle <math id="S1.SSx3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\lambda|=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\lambda|=1</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx30" title="" class="ltx_ref">LSS19b</a>]</cite>.
On the other hand, #P-hardness was showed for the arc of the circle <math id="S1.SSx3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\lambda|=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\lambda|=1</annotation></semantics></math> where Lee-Yang zeros are dense <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx8" title="" class="ltx_ref">BGPR22</a>]</cite>.
Our Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 5. ‣ Applications of our approach ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> makes a step towards an FPTAS for the ferromagnetic Ising model with non-uniform complex external fields on graphs of bounded degree based on SSM.
Are there other interesting models for which SSM based FPTAS can be obtained from zero-freeness via our approach? We leave it for further study.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SSx4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Organization</h3>

<div id="S1.SSx4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SSx4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The paper is organized as follows. In Section <a href="#S2" title="2 Preliminaries ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, we give some definitions and notations, and we introduce certain results from complex analysis.
In Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Zero-freeness implies SSM for 𝜆 ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we propose our approach and prove a Christoffel-Darboux type identity for the 2-spin system on trees. We apply the approach to show that zero-freeness implies SSM for the external field <math id="S1.SSx4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>.
In Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Zero-freeness implies SSM for 𝛽 and 𝛾 ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, we apply the same approach to show that zero-freeness implies SSM for <math id="S1.SSx4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SSx4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> in a unified way.
In Section <a href="#S5" title="5 SSM for the ferromagnetic Ising model ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, we extend our approach to deal with the 2-spin system with non-uniform external fields, and give a new SSM result for the non-uniform ferromagnetic Ising model from the Lee-Yang circle theorem.
We conclude the paper and introduce some open questions in Section <a href="#S6" title="6 Concluding remark and questions ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Preliminaries</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Notations and definitions</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a partial configuration <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\Lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\Lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>, for any <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda^{\prime}\subset\Lambda," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>⊂</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda^{\prime}\subset\Lambda,</annotation></semantics></math> we denote <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\Lambda^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\Lambda^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> the restriction of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\Lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\Lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, called a sub-partial configuration of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\Lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\Lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Consider the rational function <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)=\frac{Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda},+}_{G,v}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)}{Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">+</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)=\frac{Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda},+}_{G,v}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)}{Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)}.</annotation></semantics></math>
Note that when <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta,\gamma,\lambda{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta,\gamma,\lambda{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the marginal probability of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> being assigned to the spin <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math> in the Gibbs distribution.
However, when <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta,\gamma,\lambda\in\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta,\gamma,\lambda\in\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> has no probabilistic meaning and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)\in\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)\in\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.
For the setting <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=0</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=0</annotation></semantics></math>,
there are trivial partial configurations such that
<math id="S2.SS1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,v}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,v}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)=0</annotation></semantics></math>.
We rule these cases out as they are <em id="S2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">infeasible</em>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 6</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Feasible partial configuration)<span id="Thmtheorem6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given a 2-spin system defined on a graph <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> with parameters <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math>,
a partial configuration <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\Lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\Lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> is feasible if
<math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\Lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\Lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> does not assign any two adjacent vertices in <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> both to the spin <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=0</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\Lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\Lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> does not assign any two adjacent vertices in <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> both to the spin <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=0</annotation></semantics></math>.
We say a vertex <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V</annotation></semantics></math> is proper to a feasible partial configuration <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\Lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\Lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\notin\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∉</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\notin\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> and the partial configurations <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{+}_{\Lambda\cup\{v\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{+}_{\Lambda\cup\{v\}}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{-}_{\Lambda\cup\{v\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{-}_{\Lambda\cup\{v\}}</annotation></semantics></math>
obtained from <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\Lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\Lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> by further pinning <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math> respectively are still feasible.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 1</span></span><span id="Thmremark1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremark1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Any sub-partial configuration of a feasible partial configuration is still feasible.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 7</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Zero-freeness)<span id="Thmtheorem7.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}\subseteq\mathbb{C}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐔</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}\subseteq\mathbb{C}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> be a family of graphs. The partition function of the 2-spin systems defined on <math id="Thmtheorem7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> is said to be <em id="Thmtheorem7.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">zero-free</em> on <math id="Thmtheorem7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐔</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}</annotation></semantics></math> if for any graph <math id="Thmtheorem7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\in\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\in\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>, any feasible partial configuration <math id="Thmtheorem7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\Lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\Lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>, the partition function <math id="Thmtheorem7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="Thmtheorem7.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)\in\mathbf{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝐔</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)\in\mathbf{U}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">When the parameters <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> of a 2-spin system are fixed, we may write the partition function <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,\beta,\gamma}(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,\beta,\gamma}(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> and the rational function <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{G,\beta,\gamma,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{G,\beta,\gamma,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math>, which are both univariate functions on <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>.
Sometimes we may omit the fixed parameters <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> at the subscript for simplicity.
The definition of zero-freeness can be easily adopted to the partition function <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,\beta,\gamma}(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,\beta,\gamma}(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> with fixed <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>, and similarly to <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,\gamma,\lambda}(\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,\gamma,\lambda}(\beta)</annotation></semantics></math> with fixed <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, as well as <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,\beta,\lambda}(\gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,\beta,\lambda}(\gamma)</annotation></semantics></math> with fixed <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>.
When the three parameters are all fixed, we may write the value <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> and the value <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}</annotation></semantics></math> for simplicity.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Suppose that the partition function <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,\beta,\gamma}(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,\beta,\gamma}(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> is zero-free on some <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subseteq\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subseteq\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, the rational function <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{G,\beta,\gamma,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{G,\beta,\gamma,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> is analytic on <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> for any graph <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> and any feasible partial configuration <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\Lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\Lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Moreover, if the vertex <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> is proper to <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\Lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\Lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{G,\beta,\gamma,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}(\lambda)\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{G,\beta,\gamma,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}(\lambda)\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> since <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{G,\beta,\gamma,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda},+}(\lambda)\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">+</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{G,\beta,\gamma,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda},+}(\lambda)\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{G,\beta,\gamma,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda},-}(\lambda)\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{G,\beta,\gamma,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda},-}(\lambda)\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Notice that <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{\lambda\rightarrow 0}P_{G,\beta,\gamma,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}(\lambda)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>lim</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{\lambda\rightarrow 0}P_{G,\beta,\gamma,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}(\lambda)=0</annotation></semantics></math>.
We agree that <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{G,\beta,\gamma,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}(\lambda)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{G,\beta,\gamma,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}(\lambda)=0</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=0</annotation></semantics></math>.
If the zero-free region <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> is of the form <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(0)\backslash\{0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.222em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(0)\backslash\{0\}</annotation></semantics></math> for some complex neighbourhood <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(0)</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>, then the rational function <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{G,\beta,\gamma,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{G,\beta,\gamma,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> is analytic on the entire <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(0)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 8</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Strong spatial mixing)<span id="Thmtheorem8.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fix complex parameters <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta,\gamma,\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta,\gamma,\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\beta,\gamma)\neq(0,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\beta,\gamma)\neq(0,0)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, and a family of graphs <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The corresponding 2-spin system defined on <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> with parameters <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> is said to satisfy <em id="Thmtheorem8.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">strong spatial mixing (SSM)</em> with exponential rate <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> if there exists a constant <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> such that for any <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=(V,E)\in\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=(V,E)\in\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>,
any feasible partial configurations <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\Lambda_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\Lambda_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\Lambda_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\Lambda_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> may be different with <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and any vertex <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> proper to <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\Lambda_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\Lambda_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\Lambda_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\Lambda_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|P_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda_{1}}}-P_{G,v}^{\tau_{\Lambda_{2}}}\right|\leq Cr^{-d_{G}(v,\sigma_{\Lambda_{1}}\neq\tau_{\Lambda_{2}})}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub></msubsup></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|P_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda_{1}}}-P_{G,v}^{\tau_{\Lambda_{2}}}\right|\leq Cr^{-d_{G}(v,\sigma_{\Lambda_{1}}\neq\tau_{\Lambda_{2}})}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem8.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem8.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Here, we denote <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\Lambda_{1}}\neq\tau_{\Lambda_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\Lambda_{1}}\neq\tau_{\Lambda_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> the set <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\Lambda_{1}\setminus\Lambda_{2})\cup(\Lambda_{2}\setminus\Lambda_{1})\cup\{v\in\Lambda_{1}\cap\Lambda_{2}:\sigma_{\Lambda_{1}}(v)\neq\tau_{\Lambda_{2}}(v)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Lambda_{1}\setminus\Lambda_{2})\cup(\Lambda_{2}\setminus\Lambda_{1})\cup\{v\in\Lambda_{1}\cap\Lambda_{2}:\sigma_{\Lambda_{1}}(v)\neq\tau_{\Lambda_{2}}(v)\}</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e., the set on which <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\Lambda_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\Lambda_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\Lambda_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\Lambda_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> differ with each other), and <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{G}(v,\sigma_{\Lambda_{1}}\neq\tau_{\Lambda_{2}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{G}(v,\sigma_{\Lambda_{1}}\neq\tau_{\Lambda_{2}})</annotation></semantics></math> is the shortest distance from <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> to any vertex in <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\Lambda_{1}}\neq\tau_{\Lambda_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\Lambda_{1}}\neq\tau_{\Lambda_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Note that the SSM property implies the partition function <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{G}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{G}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}</annotation></semantics></math> is zero-free on the fixed point <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> since <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|P_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}|&lt;\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|P_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}|&lt;\infty</annotation></semantics></math> for any graph <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> and any feasible partial configuration <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\Lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\Lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The above SSM definition for complex parameters is stronger than the usual definition for real parameters (see Definition 5 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx27" title="" class="ltx_ref">LLY13</a>]</cite> for example).
For real values, by monotonicity one need only consider the case that <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda_{1}=\Lambda_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda_{1}=\Lambda_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> (the two configurations are on the same set of vertices).
However when defining SSM for complex parameters, it is necessary to consider the case that <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda_{1}\neq\Lambda_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda_{1}\neq\Lambda_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>
in order to ensure that the SSM-based Weitz’s algorithm works for complex parameters (see Lemma 3.4 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx44" title="" class="ltx_ref">SS21</a>]</cite>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Weitz’s algorithm and self-avoiding walk tree</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a graph <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=(V,E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=(V,E)</annotation></semantics></math> and a vertex <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V</annotation></semantics></math>,
the SAW tree of <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> denoted by <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\text{SAW}}(G,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>SAW</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\text{SAW}}(G,v)</annotation></semantics></math>, is a tree with the root <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> that
enumerates all self-avoiding paths beginning from <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>.
Additional vertices closing cycles of <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> are added as leaves of the tree.
Each vertex in <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is mapped to some vertices of <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\text{SAW}}(G,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>SAW</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\text{SAW}}(G,v)</annotation></semantics></math>.
A vertex set <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda\subseteq V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda\subseteq V</annotation></semantics></math> is mapped to some <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda_{\text{SAW}}\subseteq V_{\text{SAW}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mtext>SAW</mtext></msub><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mtext>SAW</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda_{\text{SAW}}\subseteq V_{\text{SAW}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
and a partial configuration <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\Lambda}:\Lambda\to\{\pm\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">±</mo><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\Lambda}:\Lambda\to\{\pm\}</annotation></semantics></math> is mapped to a partial configuration <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{\prime}_{\Lambda_{\text{SAW}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mtext>SAW</mtext></msub><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{\prime}_{\Lambda_{\text{SAW}}}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda_{\text{SAW}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mtext>SAW</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda_{\text{SAW}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\Lambda_{\text{SAW}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mtext>SAW</mtext></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\Lambda_{\text{SAW}}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the set of leaf vertices <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\text{SAW}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mtext>SAW</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\text{SAW}}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> closes a cycle and no vertices in the path between the root <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> and the leaf <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> are pinned by <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{\prime}_{\Lambda_{\text{SAW}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mtext>SAW</mtext></msub><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{\prime}_{\Lambda_{\text{SAW}}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For every vertex in <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\Lambda_{\text{SAW}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mtext>SAW</mtext></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\Lambda_{\text{SAW}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, a configuration is imposed to it depending on the orientation of the cycle, where the order of indices of vertices is arbitrarily chosen (see Figure <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 2.2 Weitz’s algorithm and self-avoiding walk tree ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> for an example).
We denote by <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\Lambda_{\text{SAW}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mtext>SAW</mtext></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\Lambda_{\text{SAW}}}</annotation></semantics></math> the partial configuration <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{\prime}_{\Lambda_{\text{SAW}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mtext>SAW</mtext></msub><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{\prime}_{\Lambda_{\text{SAW}}}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda_{\text{SAW}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mtext>SAW</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda_{\text{SAW}}</annotation></semantics></math> together with the imposed configuration on <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\Lambda_{\text{SAW}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mtext>SAW</mtext></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\Lambda_{\text{SAW}}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
One can easily check that if <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\Lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\Lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> is feasible in <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> is proper to <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\Lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\Lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\Lambda_{\text{SAW}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mtext>SAW</mtext></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\Lambda_{\text{SAW}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is feasible in <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\text{SAW}}(G,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>SAW</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\text{SAW}}(G,v)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> is proper to <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\Lambda_{\text{SAW}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mtext>SAW</mtext></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\Lambda_{\text{SAW}}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Here is the key result for the SAW tree construction.</p>
</div>
