<div id="Thmtheorem9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 9</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=(V,E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=(V,E)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmtheorem9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmtheorem9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=T_{\mathrm{SAW}}(G,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>SAW</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=T_{\mathrm{SAW}}(G,v)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Suppose that <math id="Thmtheorem9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\Lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\Lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> is a feasible partial configuration on some <math id="Thmtheorem9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="Thmtheorem9.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> is proper to <math id="Thmtheorem9.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\Lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\Lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmtheorem9.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\Lambda_{\mathrm{SAW}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>SAW</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\Lambda_{\mathrm{SAW}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the corresponding feasible partial configuration on <math id="Thmtheorem9.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda_{\mathrm{SAW}}\subseteq V_{\mathrm{SAW}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>SAW</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>SAW</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda_{\mathrm{SAW}}\subseteq V_{\mathrm{SAW}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then,</span></p>
<ol id="S2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{dist}_{G}(v,S)=\mathrm{dist}_{T}(v,S_{\rm{SAW}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>dist</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>dist</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>SAW</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{dist}_{G}(v,S)=\mathrm{dist}_{T}(v,S_{\rm{SAW}})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for any </span><math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\subseteq V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\subseteq V</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, the </span><math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-neighborhood </span><math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{G}(v,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{G}(v,k)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> of </span><math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> in </span><math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is mapped to </span><math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{T}(v,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{T}(v,k)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> in </span><math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, and the maximum degree of </span><math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is equal to that of </span><math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;padding-top:-2.2pt;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If there exists a region </span><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subset\mathbb{C}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subset\mathbb{C}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> such that </span><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda_{\mathrm{SAW}}}}_{T}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>T</mi><msub><mi>σ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>SAW</mi></msub></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda_{\mathrm{SAW}}}}_{T}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> on </span><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> (i.e., </span><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{T,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda_{\rm{SAW}}}}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)\neq\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>SAW</mi></msub></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{T,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda_{\rm{SAW}}}}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)\neq\infty</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">), then </span><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{T,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda_{\rm{SAW}}}}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)=P_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>SAW</mi></msub></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{T,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda_{\rm{SAW}}}}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)=P_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> on </span><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">The first property was proved by Weitz (Theorem 3.1 in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei06</a>]</cite>).</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">The second property was first proved for the hard-core model with a positive external field <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e., <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>) by Weitz <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei06</a>]</cite>, and
recently proved for the Ising model with a positive interaction parameter <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> and a nonzero complex external field <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e., <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=\gamma&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=\gamma&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in\mathbb{C}\backslash\{0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in\mathbb{C}\backslash\{0\}</annotation></semantics></math>) by Peters and Regts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx38" title="" class="ltx_ref">PR20</a>]</cite>.
The current result for the general 2-spin system with complex parameters <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> can be proved directly
following the proof of Proposition 26 in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx38" title="" class="ltx_ref">PR20</a>]</cite>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>Tools from complex analysis</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For some <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{0}\in\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{0}\in\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, we denote the open disk <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{z\in\mathbb{C}:|z-z_{0}|&lt;\rho\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{z\in\mathbb{C}:|z-z_{0}|&lt;\rho\}</annotation></semantics></math> centered at <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> of radius <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{D}_{\rho}(z_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{D}_{\rho}(z_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>, its boundary by <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial\mathbb{D}_{\rho}(z_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial\mathbb{D}_{\rho}(z_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>, and its closure by <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathbb{D}_{\rho}}(z_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathbb{D}_{\rho}}(z_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>.
For simplicity, we write <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{D}_{\rho}(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{D}_{\rho}(0)</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{D}_{\rho}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{D}_{\rho}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For a holomorphic (or analytic) function <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> on a complex neighborhood <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subseteq\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subseteq\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S2.SS3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> can be expanded as a convergent series <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(z)=\sum_{k\geq 0}a_{k}(z-z_{0})^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(z)=\sum_{k\geq 0}a_{k}(z-z_{0})^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> near <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>,
called the Taylor expansion series of <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 10</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(c.f. Theorem 4.4 in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx43" title="" class="ltx_ref">SS10</a>]</cite>)<span id="Thmtheorem10.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose <math id="Thmtheorem10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is holomorphic on an open set <math id="Thmtheorem10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subseteq\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subseteq\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="Thmtheorem10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔻</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{D}</annotation></semantics></math> is a disc centered at <math id="Thmtheorem10.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> whose closure is contained in <math id="Thmtheorem10.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>, then the Taylor series of <math id="Thmtheorem10.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="Thmtheorem10.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> converges uniformly and equals to <math id="Thmtheorem10.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="Thmtheorem10.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔻</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{D}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 11</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Cauchy integral formula)<span id="Thmtheorem11.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem11.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose <math id="Thmtheorem11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is holomorphic on an open set <math id="Thmtheorem11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subseteq\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subseteq\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="Thmtheorem11.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔻</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{D}</annotation></semantics></math> is a disc centered at <math id="Thmtheorem11.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> whose closure is contained in <math id="Thmtheorem11.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>,
then for every <math id="Thmtheorem11.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in\mathbb{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝔻</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in\mathbb{D}</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{(n)}(a)=\frac{n!}{2\pi i}\int_{S}\frac{f(z)}{(z-a)^{n+1}}\mathrm{d}z," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi>S</mi></msub><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{(n)}(a)=\frac{n!}{2\pi i}\int_{S}\frac{f(z)}{(z-a)^{n+1}}\mathrm{d}z,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem11.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem11.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmtheorem11.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=\partial\mathbb{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>𝔻</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=\partial\mathbb{D}</annotation></semantics></math> be oriented counterclockwise.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The next important tool is Montel theorem.
For more details and a proof, please refer to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx35" title="" class="ltx_ref">NN12</a>]</cite> for the version of univariate functions and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sch05</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dov21</a>]</cite> for multivariate functions.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 12</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Normal family)<span id="Thmtheorem12.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem12.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem12.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subseteq\mathbb{C}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subseteq\mathbb{C}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> be an open set. A family <math id="Thmtheorem12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> of holomorphic functions <math id="Thmtheorem12.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:U\to\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:U\to\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math> is said to be <span id="Thmtheorem12.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">normal</span> if for each infinite sequence of functions of <math id="Thmtheorem12.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>, either the sequence has a sub-sequence converging locally uniformly to a holomorphic function or the sequence itself converges locally uniformly to <math id="Thmtheorem12.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\infty</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="Thmtheorem12.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem12.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem12.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem12.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A sequence of functions converges locally uniformly to <math id="Thmtheorem12.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="Thmtheorem12.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\infty</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="Thmtheorem12.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> if for every <math id="Thmtheorem12.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\in U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\in U</annotation></semantics></math> there exists an open disk <math id="Thmtheorem12.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{D}_{\rho}(z)\subseteq U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{D}_{\rho}(z)\subseteq U</annotation></semantics></math> centered at <math id="Thmtheorem12.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="Thmtheorem12.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> such that the sequence converges uniformly to <math id="Thmtheorem12.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="Thmtheorem12.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\infty</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="Thmtheorem12.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{D}_{\rho}(z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{D}_{\rho}(z)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem13" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 13</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Montel Theorem)<span id="Thmtheorem13.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem13.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem13.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem13.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subseteq\mathbb{C}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subseteq\mathbb{C}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> be a connected open set and <math id="Thmtheorem13.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> be a family of holomorphic functions <math id="Thmtheorem13.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:U\to\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:U\to\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="Thmtheorem13.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(U)\subseteq\mathbb{C}\setminus\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(U)\subseteq\mathbb{C}\setminus\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="Thmtheorem13.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\in\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\in\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="Thmtheorem13.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> is normal.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The following Riemann mapping theorem is the key tool to map any bounded complex region to the unit open disk.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem14" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 14</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem14.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Riemann mapping theorem)<span id="Thmtheorem14.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem14.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem14.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem14.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem14.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> be a simply connected complex neighborhood of some <math id="Thmtheorem14.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{0}\in\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{0}\in\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>.
There exists a unique bijective holomorphic function, also called a conformal map <math id="Thmtheorem14.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\mathbb{D}_{1}(z_{0})\to U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\mathbb{D}_{1}(z_{0})\to U</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="Thmtheorem14.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(z_{0})=z_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(z_{0})=z_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem14.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\prime}(z_{0})&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\prime}(z_{0})&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. The inverse of <math id="Thmtheorem14.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is also holomorphic.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Zero-freeness implies SSM for <math id="S3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math></h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the section, we prove our main result that zero-freeness implies SSM.
As an example, we will fix <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta,\gamma\in\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta,\gamma\in\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, and view the partition function <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{G}(\lambda)=Z_{G,\beta,\gamma}(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{G}(\lambda)=Z_{G,\beta,\gamma}(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> as a univariate function on <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>.
We show that if <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{G}(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{G}(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> is zero-free on a complex neighborhood <math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> except for the point <math id="S3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>, then the 2-spin system exhibits SSM for any <math id="S3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in U\backslash\{0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in U\backslash\{0\}</annotation></semantics></math>. The implication from zero-freeness to SSM for the multivariate version in a complex neighborhood of <math id="S3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\gamma=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\gamma=1</annotation></semantics></math> can be proved similarly in a unified way. More details will be given in Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Zero-freeness implies SSM for 𝛽 and 𝛾 ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Proof outline</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In order to prove the main result, we define two key properties: local dependence of coefficients (LDC, Definition <a href="#Thmtheorem15" title="Definition 15 (Local dependence of coefficients). ‣ 3.2 Local dependence of coefficients and a uniform bound on a circle ‣ 3 Zero-freeness implies SSM for 𝜆 ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>)
and uniform bound on a circle (Definition <a href="#Thmtheorem16" title="Definition 16 (Uniform bound on a circle). ‣ 3.2 Local dependence of coefficients and a uniform bound on a circle ‣ 3 Zero-freeness implies SSM for 𝜆 ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>).
The first property says that the coefficients of the first certain terms in the Taylor expansions of the two rational functions <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\sigma_{\Lambda_{1}}}_{G,v}(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\sigma_{\Lambda_{1}}}_{G,v}(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\tau_{\Lambda_{2}}}_{G,v}(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\tau_{\Lambda_{2}}}_{G,v}(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> near <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> are the same given <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\Lambda_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\Lambda_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\Lambda_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\Lambda_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> share the same structure in a local neighborhood of the vertex <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> proper to them in <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, and the second property says that there exists a circle <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial\mathbb{D}_{\rho}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial\mathbb{D}_{\rho}</annotation></semantics></math> around <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> such that the rational functions <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,v}(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,v}(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> have a uniform bound for any graph <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, any feasible partial configuration <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\Lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\Lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>, any vertex <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> proper to it, and any <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in\partial\mathbb{D}_{\rho}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">∈</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in\partial\mathbb{D}_{\rho}</annotation></semantics></math>.
These two properties suffice to establish SSM on the disk <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{D}_{\rho}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{D}_{\rho}</annotation></semantics></math> via Cauchy integral formula (Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem18" title="Lemma 18. ‣ 3.2 Local dependence of coefficients and a uniform bound on a circle ‣ 3 Zero-freeness implies SSM for 𝜆 ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a>).
This overall structure was originally introduced in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Reg23</a>]</cite> to derive SSM from zero-freeness for <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> near <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> with fixed <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=1</annotation></semantics></math> (the hard-core model).
We extend it to the multivariate partition function of 2-spin systems and make it works for <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> near <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> besides <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> near <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Then, the key of the paper is to establish the above two properties from zero-freeness of the partition function (Lemmas <a href="#Thmtheorem24" title="Lemma 24. ‣ 3.3 Zero-freeness implies local dependence of coefficients ‣ 3 Zero-freeness implies SSM for 𝜆 ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">24</span></a> and <a href="#Thmtheorem25" title="Lemma 25. ‣ 3.4 Zero-freeness implies a uniform bound on a circle ‣ 3 Zero-freeness implies SSM for 𝜆 ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">25</span></a>). In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Reg23</a>]</cite>,
since the hard-core model can be expressed as a polymer model,
by using Mayer’s cluster expansion formula for polymer models, an explicit expression was given for the Taylor series of the rational function <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,v}(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,v}(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> near <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=0</annotation></semantics></math>, and hence the LDC property was proved.
However, the 2-spin system on general graphs is not known to be expressible as polymer models except for the case that <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=1</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=1</annotation></semantics></math>, which seems to be an intrinsic obstacle for applying cluster expansion to the 2-spin system.
In this paper, we overcome this obstacle by establishing a Christoffel-Darboux type identity for 2-spin systems on trees (Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3. ‣ Our contributions ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).
It turns out that an explicit expression for the Taylor series of the rational function is unnecessary for proving LDC for the 2-spin system.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">As of the second property, a uniform bound on a circle, we apply Montel theorem to show that for all graphs <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, all feasible partial configurations <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\Lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\Lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> and all vertices <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> proper to <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\Lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\Lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>, functions <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,v}(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,v}(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> form a normal family.
The proof follows from the idea of Lemma 5 in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Reg23</a>]</cite>.
However, since we need to handle three complex parameters <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta,\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta,\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, we have to use the multivariate version of Montel theorem, the analysis of which is somewhat more complicated. Also, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Reg23</a>]</cite> actually requires a uniform bound on a compact set containing <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>, for example a disk.
In our paper, we only need a uniform bound on a circle and we do not need to consider the values <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,v}(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,v}(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> near <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>, which makes our proof slightly simplified.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, we use Riemnann mapping theorem to map any complex neighborhood of <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> to the complex unit disk centered at <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>, so that we can extend SSM from a disk to any zero-free regions containing <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=0</annotation></semantics></math> (Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem27" title="Theorem 27. ‣ 3.5 From an arbitrary region to the unit disk ‣ 3 Zero-freeness implies SSM for 𝜆 ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">27</span></a>).
We give a diagram (see Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3.1 Proof outline ‣ 3 Zero-freeness implies SSM for 𝜆 ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) to summarize the approach to derive SSM from zero-freeness.</p>
</div>
