<p id="S3.SS5.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS5.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="S3.SS5.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(f(\lambda))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(f(\lambda))</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS5.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{H}(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{H}(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> are analytic near <math id="S3.SS5.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, the coefficients of the term <math id="S3.SS5.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS5.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq k\leq d_{G}(u,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq k\leq d_{G}(u,v)</annotation></semantics></math> in the Taylor series of <math id="S3.SS5.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{\sigma_{\Lambda_{1}}}_{G,\beta,\gamma,v}(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{\sigma_{\Lambda_{1}}}_{G,\beta,\gamma,v}(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS5.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{\tau_{\Lambda_{2}}}_{G,\beta,\gamma,v}(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{\tau_{\Lambda_{2}}}_{G,\beta,\gamma,v}(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> near <math id="S3.SS5.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> are the same.
Then the result follows by Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem17" title="Lemma 17. ‣ 3.2 Local dependence of coefficients and a uniform bound on a circle ‣ 3 Zero-freeness implies SSM for 𝜆 ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a> with <math id="S3.SS5.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(z)=g^{\sigma_{\Lambda_{1}}}_{G,\beta,\gamma,v}(z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(z)=g^{\sigma_{\Lambda_{1}}}_{G,\beta,\gamma,v}(z)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS5.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q(z)=g^{\tau_{\Lambda_{2}}}_{G,\beta,\gamma,v}(z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q(z)=g^{\tau_{\Lambda_{2}}}_{G,\beta,\gamma,v}(z)</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
