<p id="S4.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">for some function <math id="S4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(\beta)</annotation></semantics></math> analytic near <math id="S4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>.
The same form of equation holds for <math id="S4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}(\beta)-P_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda},u^{-}}(\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}(\beta)-P_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda},u^{-}}(\beta)</annotation></semantics></math>.
We are done with the proof of point-to-point LDC.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Also, by Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem25" title="Lemma 25. ‣ 3.4 Zero-freeness implies a uniform bound on a circle ‣ 3 Zero-freeness implies SSM for 𝜆 ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">25</span></a>, for any circle around <math id="S4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>, we can get a uniform bound for <math id="S4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,v}(\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,v}(\beta)</annotation></semantics></math> on it.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem31" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem31.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 31</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem31.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Uniform bound on a circle centered at <math id="Thmtheorem31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>)<span id="Thmtheorem31.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem31.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem31.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem31.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem31.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> be
a family of graphs, <math id="Thmtheorem31.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem31.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> be quasi-neighborhoods of some <math id="Thmtheorem31.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{0},\lambda_{0}{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{0},\lambda_{0}{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math> respectively where <math id="Thmtheorem31.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\notin U_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>∉</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\notin U_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmtheorem31.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> be a neighborhood of <math id="Thmtheorem31.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/U_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/U_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Denote <math id="Thmtheorem31.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(U_{1}\times U_{2}\backslash\{(0,0)\})\times(U_{3}\backslash\{0\})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(U_{1}\times U_{2}\backslash\{(0,0)\})\times(U_{3}\backslash\{0\})</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="Thmtheorem31.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐔</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Suppose that the partition function of the 2-spin systems defined on <math id="Thmtheorem31.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> is zero-free on <math id="Thmtheorem31.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐔</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, for any <math id="Thmtheorem31.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{1}\in U_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{1}\in U_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmtheorem31.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{1}\in U_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{1}\in U_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem31.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial\mathbb{D}_{\rho}(1/\gamma_{1})\subseteq U_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial\mathbb{D}_{\rho}(1/\gamma_{1})\subseteq U_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>,
there exists an <math id="Thmtheorem31.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> such that for any <math id="Thmtheorem31.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\in\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\in\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>, any feasible partial configuration <math id="Thmtheorem31.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\Lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\Lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>, any vertex <math id="Thmtheorem31.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> proper to <math id="Thmtheorem31.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\Lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\Lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="Thmtheorem31.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|P^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,v}(\beta,\gamma_{1},\lambda_{1})|\leq M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|P^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,v}(\beta,\gamma_{1},\lambda_{1})|\leq M</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="Thmtheorem31.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\in\partial\mathbb{D}_{\rho}(1/\gamma_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">∈</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\in\partial\mathbb{D}_{\rho}(1/\gamma_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Moreover, if <math id="Thmtheorem31.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> is a family of graphs with bounded degree, then <math id="Thmtheorem31.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem31.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> can be relaxed to be any non-zero complex numbers.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Take the compact set <math id="S4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> in Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem25" title="Lemma 25. ‣ 3.4 Zero-freeness implies a uniform bound on a circle ‣ 3 Zero-freeness implies SSM for 𝜆 ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">25</span></a> to be <math id="S4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial\mathbb{D}_{\rho}(1/\gamma_{1})\times\{\gamma_{1}\}\times\{\lambda_{1}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial\mathbb{D}_{\rho}(1/\gamma_{1})\times\{\gamma_{1}\}\times\{\lambda_{1}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem32" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem32.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 32</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem32.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(SSM for <math id="Thmtheorem32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>)<span id="Thmtheorem32.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem32.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem32.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem32.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem32.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> be
a family of graphs, <math id="Thmtheorem32.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem32.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> be quasi-neighborhoods of some <math id="Thmtheorem32.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{0},\lambda_{0}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{0},\lambda_{0}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> respectively where <math id="Thmtheorem32.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\notin U_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>∉</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\notin U_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmtheorem32.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> be a neighborhood of <math id="Thmtheorem32.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/U_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/U_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Denote <math id="Thmtheorem32.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(U_{1}\times U_{2}\backslash\{(0,0)\})\times(U_{3}\backslash\{0\})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(U_{1}\times U_{2}\backslash\{(0,0)\})\times(U_{3}\backslash\{0\})</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="Thmtheorem32.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐔</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Suppose that the partition function of the 2-spin systems defined on <math id="Thmtheorem32.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> is zero-free on <math id="Thmtheorem32.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐔</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, for any <math id="Thmtheorem32.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)\in\mathbf{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝐔</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)\in\mathbf{U}</annotation></semantics></math>, the corresponding 2-spin system exhibits SSM.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem32.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem32.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem32.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Moreover, if <math id="Thmtheorem32.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> is a family of graphs with bounded degree, then <math id="Thmtheorem32.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem32.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> can be relaxed to be any non-zero complex numbers.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Combine Lemmas <a href="#Thmtheorem30" title="Lemma 30. ‣ 4 Zero-freeness implies SSM for 𝛽 and 𝛾 ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">30</span></a> and <a href="#Thmtheorem31" title="Lemma 31 (Uniform bound on a circle centered at 1/𝛾). ‣ 4 Zero-freeness implies SSM for 𝛽 and 𝛾 ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">31</span></a> with Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem17" title="Lemma 17. ‣ 3.2 Local dependence of coefficients and a uniform bound on a circle ‣ 3 Zero-freeness implies SSM for 𝜆 ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a> and Riemann mapping theorem.
The proof is similar to the proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem27" title="Theorem 27. ‣ 3.5 From an arbitrary region to the unit disk ‣ 3 Zero-freeness implies SSM for 𝜆 ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">27</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Symmetrically, we can show that zero-freeness implies SSM for <math id="S4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem33" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem33.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 33</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem33.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(SSM for <math id="Thmtheorem33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>)<span id="Thmtheorem33.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem33.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem33.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem33.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem33.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> be
a family of graphs, <math id="Thmtheorem33.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem33.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> be quasi-neighborhoods of some <math id="Thmtheorem33.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{0},\lambda_{0}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{0},\lambda_{0}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> respectively where <math id="Thmtheorem33.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\notin U_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>∉</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\notin U_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmtheorem33.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> be a neighborhood of <math id="Thmtheorem33.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/U_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/U_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Denote <math id="Thmtheorem33.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(U_{1}\times U_{2}\backslash\{(0,0)\})\times(U_{3}\backslash\{0\})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(U_{1}\times U_{2}\backslash\{(0,0)\})\times(U_{3}\backslash\{0\})</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="Thmtheorem33.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐔</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Suppose that the partition function of the 2-spin systems defined on <math id="Thmtheorem33.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> is zero-free on <math id="Thmtheorem33.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐔</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, for any <math id="Thmtheorem33.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)\in\mathbf{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝐔</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)\in\mathbf{U}</annotation></semantics></math>, the corresponding 2-spin system exhibits SSM.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem33.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem33.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem33.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Moreover, if <math id="Thmtheorem33.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> is a family of graphs with bounded degree, then <math id="Thmtheorem33.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem33.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> can be relaxed to be any non-zero complex numbers.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Combining Theorems <a href="#Thmtheorem27" title="Theorem 27. ‣ 3.5 From an arbitrary region to the unit disk ‣ 3 Zero-freeness implies SSM for 𝜆 ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">27</span></a>, <a href="#Thmtheorem32" title="Theorem 32 (SSM for 𝛽). ‣ 4 Zero-freeness implies SSM for 𝛽 and 𝛾 ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">32</span></a> and <a href="#Thmtheorem33" title="Theorem 33 (SSM for 𝛾). ‣ 4 Zero-freeness implies SSM for 𝛽 and 𝛾 ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">33</span></a>, we get our main Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ Our contributions ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
In addition, for the Ising model, since <math id="S4.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\gamma=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\gamma=1</annotation></semantics></math> implies <math id="S4.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=\gamma=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=\gamma=1</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S4.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, we can show that zero-freeness implies SSM for <math id="S4.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> around <math id="S4.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S4.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem34" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem34.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 34</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem34.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(SSM for <math id="Thmtheorem34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> in the Ising model)<span id="Thmtheorem34.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem34.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem34.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem34.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem34.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> be
a family of graphs, <math id="Thmtheorem34.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> be a complex nonzero number, and <math id="Thmtheorem34.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> be a neighborhood of <math id="Thmtheorem34.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="Thmtheorem34.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Suppose that the partition function of the Ising model defined on <math id="Thmtheorem34.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> with the external field <math id="Thmtheorem34.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is zero-free on <math id="Thmtheorem34.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{U}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, for any <math id="Thmtheorem34.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\in U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\in U</annotation></semantics></math>, the corresponding Ising model exhibits SSM.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.5" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">For any complex <math id="S4.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{0}\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{0}\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math> and any rational function <math id="S4.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,v}</annotation></semantics></math>, one can easily check that <math id="S4.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,v}(1,1,\lambda_{0})=\frac{\lambda_{0}}{\lambda_{0}+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,v}(1,1,\lambda_{0})=\frac{\lambda_{0}}{\lambda_{0}+1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,v}(-1,-1,\lambda_{0})=\frac{\pm\lambda_{0}}{\pm\lambda_{0}+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>±</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>±</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,v}(-1,-1,\lambda_{0})=\frac{\pm\lambda_{0}}{\pm\lambda_{0}+1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, for all rational function <math id="S4.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,v}</annotation></semantics></math>, their values on the inputs <math id="S4.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,1,\lambda_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,1,\lambda_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(-1,-1,\lambda_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(-1,-1,\lambda_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> are both bounded.
Following the proof of Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem25" title="Lemma 25. ‣ 3.4 Zero-freeness implies a uniform bound on a circle ‣ 3 Zero-freeness implies SSM for 𝜆 ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">25</span></a>, we can prove a uniform bound on any compact set of <math id="S4.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> for any complex <math id="S4.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{0}\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{0}\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math> without requiring <math id="S4.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> to be a family of graphs with bounded degree.
The other parts of the proof are exactly the same as the proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem32" title="Theorem 32 (SSM for 𝛽). ‣ 4 Zero-freeness implies SSM for 𝛽 and 𝛾 ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">32</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>SSM for the ferromagnetic Ising model</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section,
we extend our approach to handle the 2-spin system with non-uniform external fields, and apply it to get a new SSM result for the non-uniform ferromagnetic Ising model from a corollary of Lee-Yang circle theorem on graphs with pinned vertices.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem35" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem35.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 35</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem35.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem35.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem35.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem35.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A 2-spin system defined on a graph <math id="Thmtheorem35.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=(V,E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=(V,E)</annotation></semantics></math> is said to has non-uniform external fields if vertices <math id="Thmtheorem35.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V</annotation></semantics></math> may have different external fields <math id="Thmtheorem35.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The partition function of such a system is defined to be</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex32" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex32.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="Z_{G}(\beta,\gamma,\boldsymbol{\lambda}):=\sum\limits_{\sigma:V\to\{+,-\}}w(\sigma)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝝀</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">+</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mi>w</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{G}(\beta,\gamma,\boldsymbol{\lambda}):=\sum\limits_{\sigma:V\to\{+,-\}}w(\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem35.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem35.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">with <math id="Thmtheorem35.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="w(\sigma)=\beta^{m_{+}(\sigma)}\gamma^{m_{-}(\sigma)}\prod\limits_{v\in V,\sigma(v)=+}\lambda_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>β</mi><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><msup><mi>γ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">+</mo></mrow></munder><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(\sigma)=\beta^{m_{+}(\sigma)}\gamma^{m_{-}(\sigma)}\prod\limits_{v\in V,\sigma(v)=+}\lambda_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The notations and definitions for the 2-spin system with a uniform external field can be extended to the setting with non-uniform external fields as above. In particular, the Christoffel-Darboux type identity (Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem23" title="Theorem 23 (Theorem restated). ‣ 3.3 Zero-freeness implies local dependence of coefficients ‣ 3 Zero-freeness implies SSM for 𝜆 ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23</span></a>) is also valid for the 2-spin system with non-uniform external fields by replacing <math id="S5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda^{d(u,v)+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda^{d(u,v)+1}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\prod_{w\in p_{uv}}\lambda_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\prod_{w\in p_{uv}}\lambda_{w}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{uv}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{uv}</annotation></semantics></math> is the unique path connecting <math id="S5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> in a tree.
The SAW tree construction also works where all vertices in <math id="S5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\text{SAW}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mtext>SAW</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\text{SAW}}</annotation></semantics></math> mapped from a single vertex <math id="S5.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V</annotation></semantics></math> have the same external field as the external field <math id="S5.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> of the original <math id="S5.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>, and
Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem9" title="Theorem 9. ‣ 2.2 Weitz’s algorithm and self-avoiding walk tree ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> still holds for the multivariate rational functions <math id="S5.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}(\beta,\gamma,\boldsymbol{\lambda})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝝀</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}(\beta,\gamma,\boldsymbol{\lambda})</annotation></semantics></math>.
For non-uniform external fields <math id="S5.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\lambda}=(\lambda_{v})_{v\in V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝝀</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\lambda}=(\lambda_{v})_{v\in V}</annotation></semantics></math>, we say they have the same argument <math id="S5.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S5.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\arg(\lambda_{v})=\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\arg(\lambda_{v})=\theta</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S5.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem36" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem36.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 36</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem36.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem36.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem36.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem36.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fix <math id="Thmtheorem36.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta,\gamma,\lambda^{\ast}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta,\gamma,\lambda^{\ast}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and a family of graphs <math id="Thmtheorem36.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Suppose that for any <math id="Thmtheorem36.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\in\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\in\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> and any feasible partial configuration <math id="Thmtheorem36.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\Lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\Lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>, the partition function <math id="Thmtheorem36.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,\beta,\gamma}(\boldsymbol{\lambda})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝛌</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,\beta,\gamma}(\boldsymbol{\lambda})</annotation></semantics></math> of the 2-spin system with non-uniform external fields <math id="Thmtheorem36.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝛌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> having the same argument <math id="Thmtheorem36.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> and satisfying <math id="Thmtheorem36.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;|\lambda_{v}|&lt;\lambda^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;|\lambda_{v}|&lt;\lambda^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="Thmtheorem36.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V(G)</annotation></semantics></math> is always non-zero.
Then, the corresponding 2-spin system
specified by <math id="Thmtheorem36.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta,\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta,\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem36.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝛌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> exhibits SSM.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Sine the SAW tree construction and Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem9" title="Theorem 9. ‣ 2.2 Weitz’s algorithm and self-avoiding walk tree ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> still hold for the 2-spin system with non-uniform external fields, we only need to consider the 2-spin system on trees.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">For a graph <math id="S5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, a feasible partial configuration <math id="S5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\Lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\Lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> together with a vertex <math id="S5.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> proper to it, and some fixed values <math id="S5.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝝀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> of external fields satisfying <math id="S5.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\arg(\lambda_{v})=\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\arg(\lambda_{v})=\theta</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S5.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;|\lambda_{v}|&lt;\lambda^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;|\lambda_{v}|&lt;\lambda^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S5.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V(G)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sup_{v\in V(G)}|\lambda_{v}|&lt;\lambda^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>sup</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sup_{v\in V(G)}|\lambda_{v}|&lt;\lambda^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math>, we define the function <math id="S5.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}(z)=P_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}(z\boldsymbol{\lambda})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝝀</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}(z)=P_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}(z\boldsymbol{\lambda})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S5.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\in\mathbb{D}_{\rho}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\in\mathbb{D}_{\rho}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S5.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=\frac{\lambda^{*}}{\sup|\lambda_{v}|}&gt;1." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mo rspace="0em">sup</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=\frac{\lambda^{*}}{\sup|\lambda_{v}|}&gt;1.</annotation></semantics></math>
Note that <math id="S5.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}(z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}(z)</annotation></semantics></math> is a univariate function on the complex variable <math id="S5.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since the partition function
<math id="S5.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,\beta,\gamma}(\boldsymbol{\lambda})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝀</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G,\beta,\gamma}(\boldsymbol{\lambda})</annotation></semantics></math> is zero-free, the function <math id="S5.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}(z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}(z)</annotation></semantics></math> avoids <math id="S5.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.p4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{D}_{\rho}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{D}_{\rho}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Fix the values of <math id="S5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝝀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Still by Montel theorem, for any graph <math id="S5.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, any feasible partial configuration <math id="S5.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\Lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\Lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>, and any vertex <math id="S5.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> proper to it, the functions <math id="S5.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}(z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}(z)</annotation></semantics></math> form a normal family.
Also, all functions in the family take a value in the interval <math id="S5.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S5.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z=e^{-i\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z=e^{-i\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Still similar to the proof Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem25" title="Lemma 25. ‣ 3.4 Zero-freeness implies a uniform bound on a circle ‣ 3 Zero-freeness implies SSM for 𝜆 ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">25</span></a>, we can show that for any <math id="S5.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\rho_{0}&lt;\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\rho_{0}&lt;\rho</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a uniform bound for the family of functions <math id="S5.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}(z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}(z)</annotation></semantics></math> on the circle <math id="S5.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial\mathbb{D}_{\rho_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>𝔻</mi><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial\mathbb{D}_{\rho_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Also, by the Christoffel-Darboux type identity (Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem23" title="Theorem 23 (Theorem restated). ‣ 3.3 Zero-freeness implies local dependence of coefficients ‣ 3 Zero-freeness implies SSM for 𝜆 ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23</span></a>) for the 2-spin system with non-uniform external fields in which <math id="S5.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda^{d(u,v)+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda^{d(u,v)+1}</annotation></semantics></math> is replace by <math id="S5.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\prod_{w\in p_{uv}}\lambda_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\prod_{w\in p_{uv}}\lambda_{w}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S5.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{uv}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{uv}</annotation></semantics></math> is the unique path connecting <math id="S5.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</span></p>
</div>
