<p id="S5.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">for some functions <math id="S5.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(z)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\prime}(z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\prime}(z)</annotation></semantics></math> analytic near <math id="S5.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>.
The same form of equation holds for <math id="S5.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}(z)-P_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda},u^{-}}(z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}(z)-P_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda},u^{-}}(z)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Similarly, we can define point-to-point LDC and LDC for functions <math id="S5.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}(z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}(z)</annotation></semantics></math> near <math id="S5.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z=0</annotation></semantics></math>, and show that point-to-point LDC implies LDC.
Thus, the functions <math id="S5.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}(z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{G,v}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}(z)</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy LDC.
Combine the uniform bound on a circle and LDC, and we get SSM by Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem17" title="Lemma 17. ‣ 3.2 Local dependence of coefficients and a uniform bound on a circle ‣ 3 Zero-freeness implies SSM for 𝜆 ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p8.1" class="ltx_p">The following is the celebrated Lee-Yang circle theorem.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem37" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem37.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 37</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem37.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Lee-Yang circle theorem)<span id="Thmtheorem37.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem37.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem37.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem37.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fix <math id="Thmtheorem37.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta&gt;1." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta&gt;1.</annotation></semantics></math>
For any graph <math id="Thmtheorem37.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=(V,E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=(V,E)</annotation></semantics></math>, the partition function <math id="Thmtheorem37.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{G}(\boldsymbol{\lambda})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝛌</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{G}(\boldsymbol{\lambda})</annotation></semantics></math> of the ferromagnetic Ising model specified by <math id="Thmtheorem37.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> and non-uniform external fields <math id="Thmtheorem37.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\lambda}=(\lambda_{v})_{v\in V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝛌</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\lambda}=(\lambda_{v})_{v\in V}</annotation></semantics></math> is nonzero if for all <math id="Thmtheorem37.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="Thmtheorem37.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\lambda_{v}|&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\lambda_{v}|&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="Thmtheorem37.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\lambda_{v}|&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\lambda_{v}|&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Note that Lee-Yang circle theorem gives zero-freeness for the partition function of the Ising model regarding the external fields <math id="S5.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝝀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, we need to apply Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem36" title="Lemma 36. ‣ 5 SSM for the ferromagnetic Ising model ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">36</span></a>, instead of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem34" title="Theorem 34 (SSM for 𝛽 in the Ising model). ‣ 4 Zero-freeness implies SSM for 𝛽 and 𝛾 ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">34</span></a> which requires zero-freeness regarding the edge activity <math id="S5.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math><span id="footnote5" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">5</span>
            
            
            
          Zeros of the partition function of the Ising model regarding <math id="footnote5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> are known as Fisher zeros <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fis65</a>]</cite>.</span></span></span>.
However, Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem36" title="Lemma 36. ‣ 5 SSM for the ferromagnetic Ising model ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">36</span></a> requires the zero-freeness condition holds for the 2-spin system on graphs even conditioning on some partial configurations.
Thus, Lee-Yang circle theorem cannot be directly adapted to our setting since it holds only for graphs with no pinned vertices.
In order to obtain SSM from zero-freeness,
we first give the following corollary
of Lee-Yang circle theorem.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem38" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem38.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 38</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem38.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem38.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem38.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem38.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fix <math id="Thmtheorem38.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> and integer <math id="Thmtheorem38.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>.
For any graph <math id="Thmtheorem38.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=(V,E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=(V,E)</annotation></semantics></math> of degree at most <math id="Thmtheorem38.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> and any feasible partial configuration <math id="Thmtheorem38.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\Lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\Lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>, the partition function <math id="Thmtheorem38.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G}(\boldsymbol{\lambda})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝛌</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G}(\boldsymbol{\lambda})</annotation></semantics></math> of the ferromagnetic Ising model specified by <math id="Thmtheorem38.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> and non-uniform external fields <math id="Thmtheorem38.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\lambda}=(\lambda_{v})_{v\in V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝛌</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\lambda}=(\lambda_{v})_{v\in V}</annotation></semantics></math> is nonzero if for all <math id="Thmtheorem38.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmtheorem38.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\lambda_{v}|&gt;\beta^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><msup><mi>β</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\lambda_{v}|&gt;\beta^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="Thmtheorem38.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;|\lambda_{v}|&lt;1/\beta^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>β</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;|\lambda_{v}|&lt;1/\beta^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">Consider the partition function <math id="S5.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{+}_{G,v}(\beta,\boldsymbol{\lambda})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝝀</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{+}_{G,v}(\beta,\boldsymbol{\lambda})</annotation></semantics></math> of the Ising model with non-uniform external fields on a graph <math id="S5.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=(V,E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=(V,E)</annotation></semantics></math> with a vertex <math id="S5.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> pinned to <math id="S5.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that</span></p>
