</div>
<div id="S5.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p12.1" class="ltx_p">Combining Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem36" title="Lemma 36. ‣ 5 SSM for the ferromagnetic Ising model ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">36</span></a> and Corollary <a href="#Thmtheorem38" title="Corollary 38. ‣ 5 SSM for the ferromagnetic Ising model ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">38</span></a>, we get a new SSM result for the ferromagnetic Ising model with non-uniform external fields.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem39" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem39.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 39</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem39.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 5. ‣ Applications of our approach ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> restated)<span id="Thmtheorem39.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem39.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem39.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem39.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fix <math id="Thmtheorem39.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> and integer <math id="Thmtheorem39.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>.
The ferromagnetic Ising model on graphs <math id="Thmtheorem39.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> of degree at most <math id="Thmtheorem39.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> specified by <math id="Thmtheorem39.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> and non-uniform positive external fields <math id="Thmtheorem39.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝛌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> with the same argument <math id="Thmtheorem39.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> exhibits SSM if <math id="Thmtheorem39.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\inf_{v\in V(G)}|\lambda_{v}|&gt;\beta^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>inf</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><msup><mi>β</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\inf_{v\in V(G)}|\lambda_{v}|&gt;\beta^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="Thmtheorem39.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sup_{v\in V(G)}|\lambda_{v}|&lt;1/\beta^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>sup</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>β</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sup_{v\in V(G)}|\lambda_{v}|&lt;1/\beta^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In particular, for a complex uniform <math id="Thmtheorem39.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, SSM holds if <math id="Thmtheorem39.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\lambda|&gt;\beta^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><msup><mi>β</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\lambda|&gt;\beta^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="Thmtheorem39.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;|\lambda|&lt;1/\beta^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>β</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;|\lambda|&lt;1/\beta^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text">We first consider the case <math id="S5.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sup_{v\in V(G)}\lambda_{v}&lt;1/\beta^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>sup</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>β</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sup_{v\in V(G)}\lambda_{v}&lt;1/\beta^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In this case, zero-freeness is guaranteed by Corollary <a href="#Thmtheorem38" title="Corollary 38. ‣ 5 SSM for the ferromagnetic Ising model ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">38</span></a>, and then SSM follows by Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem36" title="Lemma 36. ‣ 5 SSM for the ferromagnetic Ising model ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">36</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text">For the case that <math id="S5.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\inf_{v\in V(G)}\lambda_{v}&gt;\beta^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>inf</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><msup><mi>β</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\inf_{v\in V(G)}\lambda_{v}&gt;\beta^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, notice that by switching the meanings of <math id="S5.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math> spins,
the <em id="S5.p14.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">spin reversal symmetry</em> holds:
For any graph <math id="S5.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=(V,E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=(V,E)</annotation></semantics></math> and
any partial configuration <math id="S5.p14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\Lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\Lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex39" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{G}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}(\beta,\gamma,\boldsymbol{\lambda})=Z_{G}^{\overline{\sigma}_{\Lambda}}(\gamma,\beta,\boldsymbol{\lambda}^{-1})\prod_{v\in V}\lambda_{v}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝝀</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝝀</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{G}^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}(\beta,\gamma,\boldsymbol{\lambda})=Z_{G}^{\overline{\sigma}_{\Lambda}}(\gamma,\beta,\boldsymbol{\lambda}^{-1})\prod_{v\in V}\lambda_{v}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p14.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p14.2.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="S5.p14.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\lambda}^{-1}=(\lambda_{v}^{-1})_{v\in V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝝀</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mi>v</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\lambda}^{-1}=(\lambda_{v}^{-1})_{v\in V}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p14.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\sigma}_{\Lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\sigma}_{\Lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> is the partial configuration on <math id="S5.p14.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S5.p14.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\sigma}_{\Lambda}(w)=\overline{\sigma_{\Lambda}(w)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\sigma}_{\Lambda}(w)=\overline{\sigma_{\Lambda}(w)}</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e., the spin <math id="S5.p14.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\Lambda}(w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\Lambda}(w)</annotation></semantics></math> imposed to <math id="S5.p14.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> is flipped).
Thus, the Ising model with non-uniform external fields <math id="S5.p14.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝝀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> can be transferred to the Ising model with <math id="S5.p14.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\lambda}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝝀</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\lambda}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since <math id="S5.p14.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\inf_{v\in V(G)}\lambda_{v}&gt;\beta^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>inf</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><msup><mi>β</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\inf_{v\in V(G)}\lambda_{v}&gt;\beta^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S5.p14.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\sup_{v\in V(G)}\lambda^{-1}_{v}&lt;1/\beta^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.1389em">&lt;</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mi>v</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>β</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\sup_{v\in V(G)}\lambda^{-1}_{v}&lt;1/\beta^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, SSM also holds for the case that <math id="S5.p14.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\inf_{v\in V(G)}\lambda_{v}&gt;\beta^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>inf</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><msup><mi>β</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\inf_{v\in V(G)}\lambda_{v}&gt;\beta^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p15.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p15.1.1" class="ltx_text">Clearly, for a uniform <math id="S5.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, the condition <math id="S5.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\inf_{v\in V(G)}|\lambda_{v}|&gt;\beta^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>inf</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><msup><mi>β</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\inf_{v\in V(G)}|\lambda_{v}|&gt;\beta^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S5.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sup_{v\in V(G)}|\lambda_{v}|&lt;1/\beta^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>sup</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>β</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sup_{v\in V(G)}|\lambda_{v}|&lt;1/\beta^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is equivalent to <math id="S5.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\lambda|&gt;\beta^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><msup><mi>β</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\lambda|&gt;\beta^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S5.p15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;|\lambda|&lt;1/\beta^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>β</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;|\lambda|&lt;1/\beta^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Concluding remark and questions</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We prove that if the partition function of the 2-spin system is zero-free in a complex neighbourhood of <math id="S6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=0</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\gamma=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\gamma=1</annotation></semantics></math> containing a positive point, then the corresponding 2-spin system exhibits SSM.
By a Christoffel-Darboux type identity, it seems like that the implication from zero-free regions to SSM only works for complex neighbourhoods of <math id="S6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=0</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\gamma=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\gamma=1</annotation></semantics></math>.
A natural question is <em id="S6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">what is the magic of these two special settings of parameters <math id="S6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\gamma=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\gamma=1</annotation></semantics></math> for which the exact computation of the partition function is polynomial-time solvable?</em>
In fact, so far all currently known zero-free regions (and even all SSM results and all FPTAS results) for the 2-spin system lie in some complex neighborhoods of <math id="S6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=0</annotation></semantics></math> (up to a switch of the spins <math id="S6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>) or <math id="S6.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\gamma=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\gamma=1</annotation></semantics></math>.
We are curious whether this is always true and we make the following conjectures.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmconjecture1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conjecture">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmconjecture1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture 1</span></span><span id="Thmconjecture1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmconjecture1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmconjecture1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmconjecture1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fix <math id="Thmconjecture1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmconjecture1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> that are not both zeros.
Suppose that the partition function <math id="Thmconjecture1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G}(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G}(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> of the 2-spin system is zero-free on a complex region <math id="Thmconjecture1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, there exists a complex neighborhood <math id="Thmconjecture1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="Thmconjecture1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="Thmconjecture1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subseteq U^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subseteq U^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmconjecture1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G}(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G}(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> is zero-free on <math id="Thmconjecture1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmconjecture2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conjecture">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmconjecture2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture 2</span></span><span id="Thmconjecture2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmconjecture2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmconjecture2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmconjecture2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fix <math id="Thmconjecture2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmconjecture2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Suppose that the partition function <math id="Thmconjecture2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G}(\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G}(\beta)</annotation></semantics></math> of the 2-spin system is zero-free on a complex region <math id="Thmconjecture2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, there exists a complex neighborhood <math id="Thmconjecture2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="Thmconjecture2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="Thmconjecture2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subseteq U^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subseteq U^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmconjecture2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G}(\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G}(\beta)</annotation></semantics></math> is zero-free on <math id="Thmconjecture2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmconjecture3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conjecture">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmconjecture3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture 3</span></span><span id="Thmconjecture3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmconjecture3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmconjecture3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmconjecture3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fix <math id="Thmconjecture3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Suppose that the partition function <math id="Thmconjecture3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G}(\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G}(\beta)</annotation></semantics></math> of the Ising model is zero-free on a complex region <math id="Thmconjecture3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, there exists a complex neighborhood <math id="Thmconjecture3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="Thmconjecture3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="Thmconjecture3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="Thmconjecture3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subseteq U^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subseteq U^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmconjecture3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G}(\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{\sigma_{\Lambda}}_{G}(\beta)</annotation></semantics></math> is zero-free on <math id="Thmconjecture3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For the Ising model, a complex zero-free neighborhood of <math id="S6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=1</annotation></semantics></math> is known <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx29" title="" class="ltx_ref">LSS19a</a>]</cite>.
Also, one can check that the point <math id="S6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=-1</annotation></semantics></math> (with a fixed <math id="S6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>) satisfies the real contraction property introduced in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx44" title="" class="ltx_ref">SS21</a>]</cite> which implies that there exists a complex neighborhood of <math id="S6.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math> such that the partition function of the Ising model is zero-free on it.
Thus, for Conjecture <a href="#Thmconjecture3" title="Conjecture 3. ‣ 6 Concluding remark and questions ‣ From Zero-Freeness to Strong Spatial Mixing viaa Christoffel-Darboux Type Identity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, at least there exist complex neighborhoods of <math id="S6.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=-1</annotation></semantics></math> respectively such that the partition function of the Ising model is zero-free on them.</p>
</div>
